{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>I am no longer able, etc.</b> According to what I explained — that he perceived the departure of the soul called Moses — he said ״לֹא אוּכַל״ in the manner of their statement in Sefer haZohar (Zohar vol. 1, 217a), and this is its language: כַּד נָפְקַת נִשְׁמָתָא מִבַּר נָשׁ בְּאִינוּן (אַרְבְּעִין) [תְּלָתִין] יוֹמִין לֵית בַּר נָשׁ שַׁלִּיט בְּנִשְׁמָתֵיהּ — ״when the soul departs from a man thirty [forty] days beforehand, no man has mastery over his soul.״" "<b>Further</b>, it intends to tell them the reason that he recognized that this day his days and years were full. And although we have said that the reason follows the way of the righteous — to conceal the perception they attain beyond the people of their generation — therefore he told them the reason for his recognition through two reasons: first, ״I am no longer able to go out and come in,״ that authority was taken from me and given to Joshua. Second, that HaShem said to him, ״you shall not cross this Jordan״; from this he recognized that the days of his appointed time had drawn near." "<b>And according to</b> this way, ״and HaShem״ gave them a reason to direct [their attention to] ״this day,״ and they would understand from it the time that presents itself before him, that the remembrance of ״this day״ would relate to it, as we explained in another place." "<b>Further</b>, it intends in the juxtaposition of ״and HaShem said to me״ to the statement ״I am no longer able,״ etc., to declare the faithfulness of his servitude to the Creator, blessed be He: that even though he was no longer able to go out and come in, if HaShem were to say to him, ״cross the Jordan,״ he would gird himself and stand and serve to bring them into the land, and it would not have been prevented on account of his inability to go out and come in — but rather, HaShem said to him, ״you shall not cross,״ etc."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 31, :notes {0 "The Aramaic Zohar quotation is left untranslated as it appears in the Hebrew, with the variant readings (forty / thirty) preserved exactly as in the source. A full translation of the Zohar passage is provided in parentheses following the precedent of the Or HaChaim's own parenthetical glossing style."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:20:41.744649086Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}